// THE DESK — pattern-match INTO a foreign language, then filter what matched.
//
// Three results rest on @"numerals". Two are Church numerals computed by 01 and 02;
// one is the identity function, which is NOT a numeral.
//
//   5  ≡ lam f. lam x. f (f (f (f (f x))))     the sum      from 01-plus
//   6  ≡ lam f. lam x. f (f (f (f (f (f x))))) the product  from 02-mult
//   I  ≡ lam x. x                              not a numeral at all
//
// TWO FILTERS, DOING DIFFERENT JOBS
// ─────────────────────────────────
//   for( @lambda`lam f. lam x. ${body}` <- @"numerals" where ... )
//
//   * The PATTERN is a Foreign Language Term with a hole in a NESTED position. It matches
//     only terms shaped `lam f. lam x. _` — so the identity is rejected structurally — and
//     binds the λ-term's BODY out into Rholang. That is pattern matching into a foreign
//     language: `body` is now a RhoCalc value holding a sub-term of a λ-term.
//   * The `where` clause then decides among what matched. The substrate makes that call.
//
// WHAT IS LEFT BEHIND
// ───────────────────
// The rejected results are not consumed and not destroyed — they are still resting on
// @"numerals" when the program comes to rest.
//
// EXPECT: @"OUT" carries the body of the sum; the product and the identity still rest.

@"numerals"!(lambda`lam f. lam x. (f, (f, (f, (f, (f, x)))))`) |
@"numerals"!(lambda`lam f. lam x. (f, (f, (f, (f, (f, (f, x))))))`) |
@"numerals"!(lambda`lam x. x`) |

for(@lambda`lam f. lam x. ${body}` <- @"numerals"
    where lambda`lam f. lam x. ${body}` == lambda`lam f. lam x. (f, (f, (f, (f, (f, x)))))`) {
  @"OUT"!(lambda`${body}`)
}
